// ============================================================================
// ACCUMULATOR PATTERN WITH WHILE LOOPS
// ============================================================================
// Purpose: Demonstrates aggregation, reduction, and accumulation patterns
// Use Case: Summing values, calculating statistics, reduce operations
// Pattern: Initialize accumulator → iterate → update accumulator
//
// AI Agent Usage:
// - Use accumulator pattern when reducing a sequence to a single value
// - Common in: totals, averages, min/max finding, product calculations
// - Always initialize accumulator before the loop
// - Ensure loop condition prevents infinite loops
//
// This pattern is the foundation for:
// - Map-reduce operations
// - Fold/reduce in functional programming
// - Database aggregation queries (SUM, COUNT, AVG)
// ============================================================================

rune main() -> int {
    // =============================================================
    // PATTERN 1: Sum of Range (1 to N)
    // =============================================================
    // Mathematical Formula: Sum = n(n+1)/2
    // For n=10: Sum = 10×11/2 = 55
    // Use Case: Series summation, iterative aggregation
    
    sum := 0     // Accumulator: starts at identity element for addition (0)
    i := 1       // Loop variable: current number in sequence
    
    while (i <= 10) {
        sum := sum + i    // Accumulate: add current value to running total
        i := i + 1        // Advance: move to next number
    }
    
    say(sum)  // Output: 55
    
    // =============================================================
    // PATTERN 2: Factorial Calculation (N!)
    // =============================================================
    // Mathematical Formula: n! = n × (n-1) × (n-2) × ... × 1
    // For n=5: 5! = 5×4×3×2×1 = 120
    // Use Case: Combinatorics, permutations, probability
    
    factorial := 1    // Accumulator: starts at identity element for multiplication (1)
    n := 5            // Input: factorial to calculate
    
    // Countdown loop: process from n down to 1
    while (n > 0) {
        factorial := factorial * n    // Accumulate: multiply current value
        n := n - 1                    // Advance: countdown to zero
    }
    
    say(factorial)  // Output: 120
    
    ret 0
}

// Key Observations:
// 1. Accumulator initialization depends on operation:
//    - Addition/Subtraction: start at 0
//    - Multiplication/Division: start at 1
//    - Min finding: start at +infinity (or first element)
//    - Max finding: start at -infinity (or first element)
//
// 2. Loop direction matters:
//    - Count up: i := i + 1 (common for sum operations)
//    - Count down: n := n - 1 (common for factorial, optimization)
//
// 3. Loop guards prevent edge cases:
//    - Always check n > 0 before factorial
//    - Handle empty sequences gracefully
//
// Alternative Patterns:
// 1. For-range loop: for i in 1..11 { sum := sum + i }
// 2. Conditional accumulation: only sum even numbers
// 3. Multiple accumulators: track sum AND count simultaneously
// 4. Accumulator with break: exit early when threshold reached
